course: religion-ethics
lesson: 20
title: Paul, his letters and the way of love
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Saul hunted down the first Christians and approved the killing of Stephen. Then on the road to Damascus a bright light blinded him and Jesus spoke to him; he became a follower and was later called Paul.
explain: Paul travelled across the Roman world starting churches, and was shipwrecked, beaten and put in prison, yet he kept going. He wrote letters to the churches — to the Romans, the Corinthians, the Galatians and more — and they are now part of the New Testament.
explain: His most famous words are about love: love is patient and kind, not jealous, boastful, proud or rude, and without love even great gifts and cleverness are nothing. «These three remain: faith, hope and love. But the greatest of these is love.»
explain: Paul taught that the Church is like a body where every part is needed, that all are one in Christ — neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female — and that the Spirit grows good character like fruit.
explain: For a child today it means: no one is beyond change, nobody in the class is unimportant, nobody is worth less, and love is shown in everyday actions, not only in feelings.
fact: Saul hunted down the first Christians and approved the killing of Stephen.
fact: On the road to Damascus a bright light blinded Saul and Jesus spoke to him; he became a follower and was later called Paul.
fact: A «Damascus road» moment means a complete change of mind.
fact: Paul travelled across the Roman world starting churches, and was shipwrecked, beaten and imprisoned.
fact: Paul wrote letters to churches, such as Romans, Corinthians and Galatians, which are now part of the New Testament.
fact: 1 Corinthians 13 says love is patient and kind, and not jealous, boastful, proud or rude.
fact: Paul wrote that without love a person is only «a clanging cymbal», however great their gifts and knowledge.
fact: Paul wrote, «These three remain: faith, hope and love. But the greatest of these is love».
fact: Paul said the Church is like a body: every part, eye, hand or foot, is needed.
fact: Galatians 3:28 says, «There is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female, for you are all one in Christ Jesus».
fact: The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control (Galatians 5).
fact: Christians believe the Holy Spirit grows good character in people, like fruit on a tree.
exercise: What did Saul do before he became a follower of Jesus? | He hunted down the first Christians | He was one of the twelve disciples from the start | He was a fisherman on the lake with Peter | He was the Roman governor of Damascus
exercise: Whose killing did Saul approve? | Stephen's | Peter's | Lazarus's | John the Baptist's
exercise: What happened to Saul on the road to Damascus? | A bright light blinded him and Jesus spoke to him | He was shipwrecked on the way and had to swim to shore | He found a scroll of the Gospels | He met Peter, who argued him round
exercise: What is a «Damascus road» moment? | A complete change of mind | A long and boring journey | A sudden accident on a road | A visit to a foreign city
exercise: What does Saul's story show about people? | No one is beyond change | People never really change | Only good people can follow God | Travelling makes people wiser
exercise: Which of these happened to Paul on his journeys? | He was shipwrecked, beaten and imprisoned | He became a rich merchant selling cloth in every port | He was made emperor of Rome | He stayed safely at home
exercise: Where can Paul's letters be read today? | In the New Testament | In the Book of Genesis | Only in a museum in Rome | In the Psalms
exercise: To whom was the letter called Galatians written? | A group of churches in Galatia | Paul's own family back in Tarsus | The Roman emperor and his court | The high priest and his council in Jerusalem
exercise: Which of these does 1 Corinthians 13 say about love? | Love is patient and kind | Love is proud and strong | Love always wins arguments | Love keeps a list of wrongs
exercise: According to Paul, love is not… | Boastful or rude | Patient or kind | Gentle or humble | Hopeful or trusting
exercise: What did Paul say a person with great gifts but no love is like? | A clanging cymbal | A shining lamp on a stand | A strong tower on a hill | A full basket of bread
exercise: Which three things did Paul say remain? | Faith, hope and love | Gold, silver and bronze | Bread, wine and oil | Wisdom, power and fame
exercise: Which of the three did Paul call the greatest? | Love | Faith | Hope | Wisdom
exercise: What did Paul compare the Church to? | A body with many parts | A great stone castle with high walls | A flock of birds | A ship in a storm
exercise: What does the picture of the body teach? | Everyone is needed and no one is unimportant | The head is the only part of the body that really matters | Weak parts should be cut off | Each part should work alone
exercise: What does «neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female» teach? | All people are equal before God | Only some people can be Christians | People should forget where they come from | Men and women must do the same jobs
exercise: Which of these is a fruit of the Spirit? | Self-control | Fame | Riches | Physical strength
exercise: How do Christians believe the fruit of the Spirit grows? | The Holy Spirit grows good character in people | People buy it at church | It comes only from reading the Bible very quickly | It grows by winning competitions
test: My friend says Paul was one of the twelve disciples from the start. What do you put right? | He first hunted Christians and changed on the road to Damascus | He was the disciple who betrayed Jesus | He was the fisherman who walked on water | He was the brother of Jesus
test: «that kid was a proper bully last year, he'll never change, no chance». Which story makes you think again? | Saul, who hunted Christians and became Paul | Judas, who betrayed Jesus for thirty pieces of silver | Pilate, who went along with the shouting crowd | The traders whose tables were overturned in the Temple
test: Your sister says love is a feeling and nothing else. What does 1 Corinthians 13 suggest? | Love is shown in actions like patience and kindness | Love is only about romance and falling in love with someone | Love is the same as liking someone | Love means agreeing with everything
test: «He's the best player but he slags everyone off.» Which of Paul's sayings fits? | Without love great gifts are like a clanging cymbal | Neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free | Faith, hope and love remain | Every part of the body is needed
test: A boy says, «I'm rubbish at everything, the class doesn't need me.» Which picture of Paul's answers him? | The body where every part is needed | The bright light on the road | The shipwreck on the journey to Rome | The prison in Rome
test: A classmate who is a humanist asks, «Do you have to be religious to be patient and kind?» What is a fair answer? | No, people of every faith and none can be; Christians see it as the Spirit's fruit | Yes, only Christians can ever be kind | No, because kindness doesn't matter at all | Yes, and humanists are never patient
test: Some children say a new girl can't play because she's «not from round here». Which verse speaks against that? | «Neither Jew nor Greek… you are all one in Christ Jesus» | «Love is patient, love is kind» | «Feed my sheep» | «Peace, be still»
test: Your brother screams at the game console and throws the controller. Which fruit of the Spirit is he missing most? | Self-control | Faithfulness | Joy | Goodness
test: Which is the best reason Paul's words were written as letters? | He was far away and churches needed advice for real problems | He did not know how to preach | Letters were the only thing people read then | He wanted to hide his teaching from the churches he had started
test: A judge asks, «Why might love be greater than faith and hope?» Which answer is best reasoned? | Paul says love lasts for ever and is shown to others; some might value faith most, but love includes it | Because Paul said so, and that is all you need to say | Because faith and hope are only for old people | Because love is the easiest of the three to feel
test: Your friend says Paul gave up when he was shipwrecked and beaten. What does the story say? | He kept going and kept starting churches | He went home to Tarsus and never travelled again | He stopped believing in Jesus | He became a sailor instead
test: «put your own name in instead of love: "Sam is patient, Sam is kind, Sam is not rude"». What is this exercise for? | To see which parts of love you find hard | To learn the passage by heart faster | To prove you are better than others | To change the words of the Bible
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of Saul prior to his experience upon the road to Damascus? | He persecuted the first Christians | He served as one of the twelve apostles | He governed the province of Judaea | He was employed as a fisherman
exam: Pursuant to the First Letter to the Corinthians, chapter thirteen, identify the greatest of the three things which remain. | Love | Faith | Hope | Knowledge
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the comparison drawn by Paul between the Church and a body. | Every member is necessary and none is without importance | The head alone is of consequence | The weaker members ought to be removed | Each member ought to function independently
exam: Identify which of the following is enumerated among the fruit of the Spirit in the Letter to the Galatians, chapter five. | Self-control | Wealth | Renown | Physical strength
